mize card efficiency while retaining power for future plays.\n\nPromising options:\n- Play the **rocket** immediately? No — it\'s the strongest card in the game and should be saved to win back control later, especially when an opponent plays a high bomb.\n- Play the **bomb (♠3 ♥3 ♣3 ♦3)**? Strong, but again, bombs are powerful tools; using it early may waste its potential unless necessary.\n- Play a **quadplex set**: I have "four_with_pair" and "four_with_single" available using the four 3s. The move ♠3 ♥3 ♣3 ♦3 ♥5 ♣5 uses six cards at once (efficient) and clears small cards that could otherwise be hard to dispose of later.\n- Play a **long straight**: The 7-card straight (♦8 ♦9 ♣10 ♣J ♠Q ♠K ♦A) is excellent—it uses many cards and is likely unbeatable by a same-type combo due to length. Peasants rarely hold such long sequences. This would significantly reduce hand size.\n- Play **triple_with_pair** like ♥3 ♣3 ♦3 ♥5 ♣5? Good, but only five cards; less efficient than the quadplex or long straight.\n\nThe most efficient moves are either:\n1. **four_with_pair: ♠3 ♥3 ♣3 ♦3 ♥5 ♣5 ♠6 ♥6** — clears 8 cards including low-value ones, sets down a powerful quad-based play.\n2. **straight: ♦8 ♦9 ♣10 ♣J ♠Q ♠K ♦A** — 7 cards, smooth, hard to beat, and preserves bombs/rocket for defense.\n\nHowever, note that ♦9 is used both in the straight and in triplet ♠9♣9♦9. If I use ♦9 in the straight, I break up the 9s triplet. But I still keep ♠9 and ♣9 as a pair — manageable.\n\nBut wait: **Is ♦9 available for both?** Looking at my hand: I have ♠9, ♣9, ♦9 — yes, all three 9s. So if I use ♦9 in the straight, I can no longer play triple 9s later. However, I don\'t have a fourth 9 anyway, so triple 9s is only useful with attachments. Losing triple 9s isn’t too costly.\n\nAlternatively, playing the **four_with_pair** using 3s and attaching 5s and 6s clears the powerful bomb *and* gets rid of small distracting cards. This move uses 8 cards: four 3s, two 5s, two 6s — extremely efficient.\n\nAlso, since 3s are the lowest rank, peasants might not expect such a big play early, and they may not be able to beat this quad-with-two-pairs (which requires another bomb or rocket). It’s highly disruptive.\n\nMoreover, keeping the rocket is wise for later control.\n\nThus, between the long straight and the quad-with-pair, the latter is more aggressive and efficient, removing more cards and utilizing the bomb constructively.\n\nReason: Playing the quad-with-pair (four 3s + pair 5s + pair 6s) removes eight cards in one go, including a bomb and several low-value cards that are harder to play individually. It puts immediate pressure on the peasants, who likely cannot beat this without a bomb or rocket of their own. It also avoids fragmenting my hand into weak leftovers. The straight is good, but sacrificing the bomb here yields greater long-term advantage.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠3", "♥3", "♣3", "♦3", "♥5", "♣5", "♠6", "♥6"]}'}
